# Source for q/ae-peri-aorta-mass.js: Pericardial disease, Aorta, Masses.
PE="Pericardial disease"; AO="Aorta"; MA="Masses"
Q=[
# --- Pericardial effusion and tamponade ---
(PE,"In the parasternal long-axis view, a pericardial effusion is distinguished from a left pleural effusion because the pericardial effusion lies:","Anterior to the descending aorta",["Posterior to the descending aorta","Inside the left atrium","Only around the RV"],"Pericardial fluid tracks between the heart and the descending aorta. Pleural fluid extends behind the aorta.","F"),
(PE,"A pericardial effusion measuring 10 to 20 mm in diastole is usually graded as:","Moderate",["Trivial","Small","Large"],"Small is under 10 mm; large is over 20 mm. Size alone does not determine tamponade.","F"),
(PE,"An echo-free space seen only anteriorly, which moves with the heart and has fine internal echoes, is most likely:","An epicardial fat pad",["A large pericardial effusion","Tamponade","A pleural effusion"],"Fat is often seen anterior to the RV in older or obese patients. True effusions usually collect posteriorly first.","F"),
(PE,"Cardiac tamponade is caused by:","Pericardial pressure rising enough to restrict filling of the heart",["Thickened pericardium only","Valve stenosis","High LV pressure"],"The speed at which fluid collects matters more than the volume; a small, rapid bleed can cause tamponade.","F"),
(PE,"The earliest and most sensitive echo sign of tamponade is:","Right atrial collapse in late diastole/early systole",["LV hypertrophy","Aortic dilation","Mitral valve prolapse"],"RA collapse lasting more than a third of the cardiac cycle is more specific.","F"),
(PE,"Right ventricular collapse in tamponade occurs during:","Early diastole",["Late systole","Atrial systole only","Isovolumic contraction"],"The RV free wall is pushed inward when RV pressure is lowest. It is more specific than RA collapse.","F"),
(PE,"In tamponade, the IVC is typically:","Dilated with less than 50% collapse on inspiration (plethoric)",["Small and fully collapsing","Absent","Normal"],"A plethoric IVC is very sensitive; a normal, collapsing IVC makes tamponade unlikely.","F"),
(PE,"Respiratory variation of mitral inflow E velocity greater than about 25% to 30% in a patient with an effusion suggests:","Tamponade physiology",["Normal physiology","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Severe AS"],"Tricuspid inflow variation above about 40% to 60% also supports tamponade.","F"),
(PE,"In tamponade, during inspiration:","Right-sided filling increases while left-sided filling decreases",["Both sides increase","Both sides decrease","Left-sided filling increases"],"The heart cannot expand, so the septum shifts left when the RV fills (ventricular interdependence).","F"),
(PE,"A 'swinging heart' in a large effusion corresponds to which ECG finding?","Electrical alternans",["ST elevation in all leads only","Left bundle branch block","Atrial flutter"],"The heart swings within the fluid, changing the QRS axis beat to beat.","F"),
(PE,"Pulsus paradoxus is:","A fall in systolic blood pressure of more than 10 mmHg during inspiration",["A rise in pressure with inspiration","A pulse that alternates strong and weak","A slow-rising pulse"],"It reflects exaggerated ventricular interdependence in tamponade.","F"),
(PE,"After cardiac surgery, tamponade can be hard to recognize because:","The effusion may be loculated, compressing only one chamber",["Effusions never occur after surgery","Tamponade always shows RV collapse","The IVC is always small"],"A localized clot behind the LA or RV can cause tamponade. TEE may be needed.","F"),
(PE,"Echo-guided pericardiocentesis commonly uses which approach?","Subxiphoid or apical, whichever has the most fluid closest to the skin",["Suprasternal","Right parasternal only","Through the LV"],"Agitated saline injected through the needle confirms its position in the pericardial space.","F"),
(PE,"Agitated saline injected during pericardiocentesis that appears in the RV indicates:","The needle is in the RV cavity and must be withdrawn",["Correct position in the pericardial space","A PFO","Normal finding"],"Bubbles should appear in the pericardial fluid, not inside a cardiac chamber.","C"),
(PE,"Fibrinous strands within a pericardial effusion suggest:","An exudative, inflammatory or infectious effusion",["A simple transudate","Normal pericardium","A pleural effusion"],"Strands and loculations are common in infection, tuberculosis, malignancy and after surgery.","F"),
(PE,"Common causes of pericardial effusion include all EXCEPT:","Mitral valve prolapse",["Malignancy","Kidney failure (uremia)","Hypothyroidism"],"Others include viral pericarditis, post-MI and post-surgery, trauma and tuberculosis.","F"),
(PE,"Acute pericarditis on echo often shows:","A normal study, or a small effusion",["Always a large effusion","Thick calcified pericardium","LV hypertrophy"],"Pericarditis is a clinical diagnosis (chest pain, rub, ECG changes). Echo looks for effusion and complications.","F"),
(PE,"A hemopericardium in a patient with an acute type A aortic dissection is:","A life-threatening emergency",["A normal finding","A sign of healing","Expected after any MI"],"Rupture into the pericardium is a common cause of death in dissection.","F"),
(PE,"A thickened pericardium in constrictive pericarditis is typically more than about:","3 to 4 mm",["0.5 mm","10 cm","Pericardial thickness cannot be seen"],"Echo is limited for measuring pericardial thickness. CT or MRI is better, and some patients with constriction have normal thickness.","C"),
(PE,"Effusive-constrictive pericarditis is suspected when:","RA pressure stays high after the effusion is drained",["The effusion disappears and pressures normalize","The patient has only a pleural effusion","EF is reduced"],"The visceral pericardium is thickened, so constriction persists after drainage.","C"),
(PE,"A pericardial cyst is most often found:","At the right cardiophrenic angle",["Inside the LA","On the aortic valve","In the LV apex"],"It is a benign, fluid-filled structure next to the RA.","C"),
(PE,"Congenital absence of the left pericardium may cause:","A leftward-shifted, hypermobile heart that makes the RV look enlarged",["Tamponade","Aortic stenosis","A bicuspid aortic valve"],"Unusual windows and exaggerated motion are clues. MRI confirms it.","C"),
(PE,"Low-pressure tamponade can occur in:","Dehydrated or bleeding patients with only a modest effusion",["Patients with high RA pressure only","Patients with severe AS","Every patient with a small effusion"],"Low intracardiac pressures let even small pericardial pressures compress the chambers.","C"),
(PE,"Which view is often best for looking at an effusion and RV collapse in a ventilated ICU patient?","Subcostal",["Suprasternal","High right parasternal","Apical two-chamber"],"Subcostal windows are often the best available when patients can't be turned.","F"),
# --- Aorta ---
(AO,"The aortic root is measured in the parasternal long-axis view at:","The sinuses of Valsalva, at end-diastole",["The LVOT, at end-systole","The aortic arch","The descending aorta"],"ASE recommends leading edge to leading edge at end-diastole for root measurements.","F"),
(AO,"The normal sequence of aortic root measurements from the heart outward is:","Annulus, sinuses of Valsalva, sinotubular junction, ascending aorta",["Ascending aorta, annulus, sinuses, STJ","Sinuses, annulus, arch, STJ","Arch, sinuses, annulus, LVOT"],"Measure all four in PLAX; move up a rib space (high parasternal) for the ascending aorta.","F"),
(AO,"In Marfan syndrome, the aortic root typically shows:","Dilation at the sinuses with loss (effacement) of the sinotubular junction",["A small, normal root","Coarctation","A bicuspid valve"],"The pear or flask shape is classic. MVP and aortic regurgitation are also common.","F"),
(AO,"Stanford type A aortic dissection involves:","The ascending aorta",["Only the descending aorta","Only the abdominal aorta","The pulmonary artery"],"Type A is a surgical emergency. Type B involves the descending aorta only and is often managed medically.","F"),
(AO,"DeBakey type I aortic dissection:","Starts in the ascending aorta and extends to the arch and beyond",["Involves the ascending aorta only","Involves the descending aorta only","Involves the pulmonary artery"],"DeBakey II is limited to the ascending aorta; DeBakey III starts in the descending aorta.","C"),
(AO,"The hallmark echo sign of aortic dissection is:","A mobile intimal flap separating a true and false lumen",["A calcified valve","A pericardial cyst","A small aortic root"],"Color Doppler shows different flow in each lumen.","F"),
(AO,"The true lumen in aortic dissection usually:","Expands in systole and has brisk forward systolic flow",["Is larger with slow, swirling flow","Contains thrombus","Has no flow"],"The false lumen is usually larger with slow or absent flow and may contain thrombus.","C"),
(AO,"Complications of type A dissection that echo should look for include:","Aortic regurgitation, pericardial effusion and wall motion abnormalities",["Mitral stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","Coarctation"],"The flap can disrupt the valve or coronary arteries (often the right coronary), and rupture can cause tamponade.","F"),
(AO,"An intramural hematoma of the aorta appears as:","Crescent-shaped thickening of the aortic wall without an intimal flap",["A mobile flap","A dilated root only","A calcified plaque"],"It is bleeding into the wall and is managed like a dissection.","C"),
(AO,"Coarctation of the aorta most commonly occurs:","Just beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery",["At the aortic valve","In the abdominal aorta","At the innominate artery"],"It is best seen in the suprasternal view.","F"),
(AO,"The CW Doppler signal in significant coarctation shows:","High systolic velocity with continued forward flow into diastole (a 'diastolic tail')",["Low velocity with no diastolic flow","Flow only in diastole","A normal waveform"],"The persistent diastolic gradient indicates severe narrowing.","F"),
(AO,"Abdominal aortic Doppler below a significant coarctation shows:","Damped flow with a slow upstroke and continuous diastolic flow",["Normal triphasic flow","Holodiastolic reversal","Absent flow"],"Loss of the normal pulsatile pattern is a useful clue from the subcostal view.","C"),
(AO,"Coarctation is associated with:","Bicuspid aortic valve and Turner syndrome",["Marfan syndrome only","Ebstein anomaly","Carcinoid"],"Always check the arch in patients with a bicuspid valve.","F"),
(AO,"From the suprasternal view, the three branches of the aortic arch in order from proximal to distal are:","Innominate (brachiocephalic), left common carotid, left subclavian",["Left subclavian, left carotid, innominate","Right carotid, left carotid, innominate","Celiac, superior mesenteric, renal"],"The right pulmonary artery is seen in cross-section under the arch.","F"),
(AO,"The structure seen in cross-section beneath the aortic arch in the suprasternal long-axis view is the:","Right pulmonary artery",["Left atrium appendage","Superior vena cava","Coronary sinus"],"The left atrium lies below the right pulmonary artery.","F"),
(AO,"A sinus of Valsalva aneurysm most commonly arises from the:","Right coronary sinus",["Non-coronary sinus only","Left coronary sinus only","Pulmonary sinus"],"It can rupture into the RV or RA, producing continuous flow on Doppler.","C"),
(AO,"Rupture of a sinus of Valsalva aneurysm into the right heart produces which Doppler pattern?","Continuous flow through systole and diastole",["Diastolic flow only","Systolic flow only","No flow"],"Aortic pressure exceeds right heart pressure throughout the cycle.","C"),
(AO,"Bicuspid aortic valve aortopathy typically involves:","Dilation of the ascending aorta",["Narrowing of the abdominal aorta","Pulmonary artery dilation only","No aortic involvement"],"Measure the ascending aorta from a high parasternal window in all bicuspid patients.","F"),
(AO,"Complex aortic arch atheroma (4 mm or more, or mobile) on TEE is important because it:","Increases the risk of stroke and embolism",["Is a normal finding","Causes coarctation","Causes tamponade"],"Mobile plaque is a source of emboli during procedures such as cardiac catheterization.","C"),
(AO,"Normal adult aortic root diameter at the sinuses is usually less than about:","4.0 cm",["2.0 cm","6.0 cm","8.0 cm"],"Normal values depend on age, sex and body size. An aneurysm is generally 1.5 times the expected diameter.","F"),
(AO,"Loeys-Dietz syndrome, like Marfan syndrome, is associated with:","Aortic root aneurysm and dissection at smaller diameters",["Coarctation only","Mitral stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis"],"Arterial tortuosity and a bifid uvula are features. Surgery is often recommended earlier.","C"),
(AO,"In the PLAX view, the descending thoracic aorta appears as:","A circle behind the left atrium near the AV groove",["A circle in front of the RV","A tube above the aortic valve","A structure inside the LA"],"It is a key landmark for separating pericardial from pleural effusion.","F"),
(AO,"The abdominal aorta is best seen from the:","Subcostal window",["Suprasternal notch","Apical window","Parasternal short-axis view"],"Subcostal views show the abdominal aorta to the left of the IVC.","F"),
(AO,"A penetrating aortic ulcer is:","An atherosclerotic plaque that erodes through the intima into the aortic wall",["A congenital narrowing","A tumor","A valve defect"],"It can lead to intramural hematoma, dissection or rupture.","C"),
(AO,"TEE is useful for suspected dissection because it:","Images the thoracic aorta closely with high sensitivity, at the bedside",["Shows the abdominal aorta best","Avoids the need for any sedation","Cannot see the ascending aorta"],"A blind spot exists in the distal ascending aorta and proximal arch, where the trachea lies between esophagus and aorta.","C"),
# --- Masses ---
(MA,"The most common primary cardiac tumor in adults is:","Myxoma",["Papillary fibroelastoma","Angiosarcoma","Rhabdomyoma"],"Metastatic tumors to the heart are far more common than any primary tumor.","F"),
(MA,"A left atrial myxoma is classically attached to the:","Interatrial septum near the fossa ovalis by a stalk",["Mitral leaflet tip","LV apex","Pulmonary veins"],"Being mobile, it can prolapse into the mitral orifice in diastole.","F"),
(MA,"A large LA myxoma prolapsing through the mitral valve can mimic:","Mitral stenosis",["Aortic regurgitation","HCM","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"It obstructs inflow, raising the mitral gradient and causing symptoms that may change with position.","F"),
(MA,"The most common tumor of the heart valves is:","Papillary fibroelastoma",["Myxoma","Lipoma","Rhabdomyoma"],"It is small, mobile and frond-like ('sea anemone'), often on the aortic side of the aortic valve or the mitral valve.","F"),
(MA,"Papillary fibroelastomas are clinically important because they:","Can embolize and cause stroke",["Always cause heart failure","Are malignant","Cause coarctation"],"Surgical removal is often recommended for mobile or symptomatic tumors.","F"),
(MA,"The most common primary cardiac tumor in children is:","Rhabdomyoma",["Myxoma","Angiosarcoma","Papillary fibroelastoma"],"Rhabdomyomas are associated with tuberous sclerosis and often regress with age.","F"),
(MA,"The most common primary malignant cardiac tumor in adults is:","Angiosarcoma, usually in the right atrium",["Myxoma","Lipoma","Rhabdomyoma"],"It is aggressive and often causes pericardial effusion.","F"),
(MA,"Renal cell carcinoma can reach the heart by:","Growing up the IVC into the right atrium",["Spreading through the pulmonary veins","Crossing an ASD","Through the coronary arteries"],"Liver and adrenal tumors can extend the same way.","C"),
(MA,"Compared with primary cardiac tumors, metastases to the heart are:","Much more common",["Much rarer","Equally common","Never seen"],"Lung, breast, melanoma and lymphoma are common sources. Pericardial effusion is a frequent sign.","F"),
(MA,"Lipomatous hypertrophy of the interatrial septum appears as:","A dumbbell-shaped thickening that spares the fossa ovalis",["A mobile mass on a stalk","A mass on the aortic valve","A thin, membranous septum"],"It is a benign fat deposit, common in older and obese patients.","F"),
(MA,"Left atrial appendage thrombus is most likely in patients with:","Atrial fibrillation or mitral stenosis",["Normal sinus rhythm and normal valves","Pulmonic stenosis","Athlete's heart"],"Slow flow in the appendage promotes clot. TEE is needed to exclude it.","F"),
(MA,"Low LAA emptying velocity on TEE (under about 20 cm/s) suggests:","Higher risk of thrombus formation",["Normal function","Mitral regurgitation","Pericarditis"],"Normal LAA emptying velocity is usually over 40 cm/s.","C"),
(MA,"The pectinate muscles in the left atrial appendage can be mistaken for:","Thrombus",["Vegetations on the aortic valve","Pericardial effusion","Atrial septal defect"],"They are parallel ridges in the appendage wall. Multiple TEE views help tell them apart.","F"),
(MA,"The 'Coumadin ridge' (Q-tip sign) is:","A normal ridge between the left atrial appendage and the left upper pulmonary vein",["A thrombus","A tumor","An abscess"],"It was once mistaken for thrombus and treated with warfarin, hence the name.","F"),
(MA,"A Chiari network is:","A normal, mobile, net-like remnant in the right atrium",["A tumor","A vegetation on the tricuspid valve","A thrombus in the LV"],"It is a remnant of the embryonic sinus venosus valve and is often linked to PFO.","F"),
(MA,"The Eustachian valve is found at the junction of the:","Inferior vena cava and right atrium",["Superior vena cava and right atrium","Coronary sinus and LV","Pulmonary veins and LA"],"It directed oxygenated blood toward the foramen ovale in the fetus.","F"),
(MA,"The crista terminalis is:","A normal muscular ridge on the posterior right atrial wall",["A tumor in the RA","A thrombus","A pericardial cyst"],"It can look like a mass in the RA in some views.","F"),
(MA,"Nodules of Arantius are:","Normal small thickenings at the center of the aortic cusp free edges",["Vegetations","Tumors","Calcified emboli"],"They help the cusps seal and are more prominent with age.","C"),
(MA,"A false tendon (LV band) is:","A normal fibrous strand crossing the LV cavity",["A ruptured chord","A thrombus","A tumor"],"It is usually benign and can be mistaken for a flail chord or thrombus.","F"),
(MA,"An atrial septal aneurysm is defined by septal movement of at least:","10 mm beyond the plane of the septum",["1 mm","3 mm","30 mm only"],"It is often associated with a patent foramen ovale.","C"),
(MA,"A thrombus in the right atrium is commonly associated with:","Central venous catheters and pacemaker leads",["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Bicuspid valve"],"Clot in transit from the legs can also be seen passing through the RA, a sign of pulmonary embolism risk.","F"),
(MA,"LV apical thrombus is most often seen:","After a large anterior MI with apical akinesis or aneurysm",["In athlete's heart","With mitral stenosis","With a normal LV"],"Contrast echo improves detection.","F"),
(MA,"Contrast perfusion imaging helps tell a tumor from a thrombus because:","Tumors have a blood supply and enhance with contrast, while thrombus does not",["Thrombus enhances strongly","Neither shows any difference","Tumors never take up contrast"],"Highly vascular tumors may enhance more than the surrounding myocardium.","C"),
(MA,"Key features to describe for any cardiac mass include:","Location, attachment, size, mobility, echogenicity and effect on flow",["Only its color on Doppler","Only its size","Only whether it moves"],"These features help separate tumor, thrombus, vegetation and normal variants.","F"),
(MA,"A cardiac fibroma is typically:","A solid, non-contractile mass within the ventricular wall, often in children",["A mobile stalked mass in the LA","A valve tumor","A pericardial cyst"],"It is usually in the LV free wall or septum and can cause arrhythmias.","C"),
(MA,"Pacemaker and defibrillator leads appear on echo as:","Bright linear echoes in the right atrium and right ventricle",["Masses in the left atrium","Vegetations on the aortic valve","Flaps in the aorta"],"Leads can cause tricuspid regurgitation and can become sites of thrombus or infection.","F"),
(MA,"A thrombus in the left atrial body is most likely in a patient with:","Severe rheumatic mitral stenosis",["Normal mitral valve and sinus rhythm","Severe aortic regurgitation","Pulmonic stenosis"],"Thrombus may extend from the appendage into the body of a very large LA.","F"),
(MA,"A 'tumor plop' heard on auscultation is associated with:","A mobile atrial myxoma",["Pericarditis","Aortic stenosis","HCM"],"The sound occurs as the tumor falls into the ventricle in early diastole.","C"),
]
